        "id": "ch04.open.p01",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch04.open.p01.r01",
            "text": "In the previous chapter, the transformer block brought together layer normalization, feed-forward layers, activation, and shortcut connections; here, those pieces are assembled into the full GPT pipeline that turns tokenized text into model outputs.",
            "type": "R",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.open.p01.r02",
            "text": "This matters because the aim is not just to study isolated parts, but to assemble a fully working version of the original 124 million parameter GPT-2 model and to see how text moves through tokenization, dropout, transformer blocks, another layer normalization, and the output head.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "G3-JgHckzjw@380",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@384",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@388",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@115",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@119",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@123",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@125",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@148",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@150",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@153",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@3540",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@3542",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@3544",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@3545"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.open.p01.r03",
            "text": "It also marks the shift from architecture to training, because next-token predictions need training to make sense rather than remain random.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "G3-JgHckzjw@234",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@236",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@239",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@241",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@158",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@160",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@169",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@172",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@174",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@176"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch04.open.p02",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch04.open.p02.r01",
            "text": "We begin with what the model is and how the full GPT pipeline is assembled, then review configuration, tokenization, vocabulary, inputs, batches, embeddings, dimensions, dropout, layer normalization, and transformer-block internals.",
            "type": "R",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.open.p02.r02",
            "text": "From there, the chapter stacks blocks into the final model, implements the code, counts parameters, defines next-token targets, introduces loss and perplexity, prepares data, and walks through the basic training loop with monitoring and evaluation mode.",
            "type": "R",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.open.p02.r03",
            "text": "It closes by turning logits into text with greedy, temperature, and top-k decoding, and by noting a small narrative gap in how decoding is positioned within the chapter.",
            "type": "R",
            "cites": []
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch04.glance.p01",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch04.glance.p01.01",
            "text": "The chapter’s central build target is a fully working version of the original 124 million parameter GPT-2 model.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "G3-JgHckzjw@380",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@384",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@388"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch04.glance.p02",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch04.glance.p02.r01",
            "text": "In the GPT architecture, text is tokenized, turned into embeddings, passed through dropout and transformer blocks, and then processed by a final layer normalization and linear output head to get logits.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
